Java Constructors
----------------

Constructor is a special type of method used for initializing objects and is called automatically
whenever we create an object of a class using the new keyword.

If developer creates a constructor within the class, then Java won't provide default constructor. We have t declare one.

Constructors can be overloaded but cannon be overridden. Because Constructors belong to their own class
and have the same name as that of class name and are used to initialize objects. Then can't be inherited.

A constructor cannot be declared as final. The purpose of the final keyword is to prevent method overriding.
Constructors are never inherited or overridden because they belong only to their own class and have the same
name as the class. Since constructors cannot be overridden, declaring them as final has no meaning, so Java
does not allow it.

Constructors can be overloaded, but while running the application compiler will only search for the
method with valid signature String[] args, and it will call this method.

Constructors are not inherited because, they are used to initialize objects of a specific class.
Since they are tied to the class itself and have the same name as the class, they are not inherited by
child classes. When a child object is created, it calls the parent constructor using super(),
but it does not inherit the parent's constructor.

Constructor Chaining in Java
----------------------------

Constructor chaining is the process of calling one constructor from another constructor to reuse code and
avoid duplication. It can be done using this() within the same class or super() to call a parent class
constructor.

this() is used to call another constructor of the same class.
It must be the first statement inside the constructor.

super() is used to call the constructor of the parent class.
If we don't write it explicitly, Java inserts it automatically.
